      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, the students! They were absolutely up to their old tricks, trying to swap out Dewey numbers for LCC codes just to see if the system would catch them. It was a classic case of academic mischief, but honestly, it reminded me of a time I tried to organize a collection of goblin artifacts.\n\nYou see, when I was cataloging a hoard of goblin trinkets, I initially used a simple numbering system like the Dewey Decimal System. I thought it would be efficient, but the goblins were too clever. They would swap out the numbers based on the \"spirit\" of the item rather than its physical description. For instance, a goblin's favorite shiny button might be classified under \"General Curiosities\" (000s), but if the goblin was wearing it, the system would suddenly shift it to \"Costume History\" (790s) because the goblin's *intent* was to dress up.\n\nIt was chaotic! The goblins would even try to \"borrow\" items from other sections, leaving the cataloging system in a state of perpetual confusion. It was like trying to keep a library of goblin literature organized when the goblins themselves are the",
